    thinking: "**Envisioning Toy Landscapes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on replacing the existing environmental elements with charming Isometric Toy-Town/Claymorphism models. Specifically, I\'m starting with vegetation, envisioning fluffy, cloud-like trees and bushes. These will consist of smooth, overlapping low-poly spheres, perhaps using IcosahedronGeometry to achieve the desired effect for the spherical crowns.\n\n\n**Designing Toy Bushes, Buildings**\n\nI\'ve made progress on the toy vegetation, thinking about colors for the tree crowns, such as bright green, lime, and yellow, and smooth chocolate clay for the trunks. I\'m now moving on to the buildings. I envision pastel-colored walls with trim, multi-pane windows, and wooden doors with awnings. Details on the roof designs are in progress too, with gabled/pitched roofs and dormer windows.\n\n\n**Adding Architectural Details**\n\nI\'m now focusing on adding some more details to the buildings. I\'m imagining tiny pitched roofs with dormer windows, to add character, and also tiny chimneys with white stone caps. I\'m also thinking about Mansard-style roofs for townhouses with rooftop gardens or skylights. Finally, I\'m envisioning mid-rise buildings like boutique hotels with balconies, awnings, and pergolas on the roof. I\'m now thinking about specific landmarks, like a Cathedral, a pier, and a musical monument, all in the toy-town style. I\'m imagining a circular plaza for the monument, with mini topiary bushes. I plan to incorporate these changes into `ThreeCanvas.tsx` right away.\n\n\n"
